{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Here is seed for you.</b> He gave them seed for the five years that remained that were fit to be years of famine, but the famine ceased in the merit of Jacob (Tosefta Sotah 13). Therefore he said, 'Here is seed for you' — that is, for the five years. And the received tradition says that from the final ה of the Name all offspring and every thing yielding fruit were drawn forth. And so that the matter would serve as a remembrance, he commanded them to give a fifth to Pharaoh and to keep four parts for themselves. And there is a further allusion, that all this was in the merit of Jacob: for just as Jacob did not eat from all that was his except four parts, and set aside a fifth part for the One above — as it says, (Genesis 28:22) ״וְכֹל אֲשֶׁר תִּתֶּן לִי עַשֵּׂר אֲעַשְּׂרֶנּוּ לָךְ״ — which is two tithes, and the latter tithe is like the former tithe, and that is the fifth — so too all who ate by his merit he laid upon them this obligation, that they would eat only four parts and set aside the fifth. And likewise at the beginning of the famine, ״וְחִמֵּשׁ אֶת אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ (Genesis 41:34) — that he took from all of them a fifth part, for there HaShem commanded the blessing, as it is written, (Deuteronomy 14:22) ״עַשֵּׂר תְּעַשֵּׂר״ — two tithes. And in this matter it is permitted to test HaShem, as it says, (Malachi 3:10) ״וּבְחָנוּנִי נָא בָּזֹאת״ etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase עִשּׂוּרָא בַּתְרָא כְּעִשּׂוּרָא קַמָּא דָּמֵי ('the latter tithe is like the former tithe') is a known rabbinic dictum (e.g., b. Bekhorot 53a) rendered literally here; the logic is that two tithes (a tenth and a tenth) equal a fifth."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:59.135648558Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}